{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at exact clean pushed head f61340c01c157bf82cbddf3792fa3075e924d362 (branch request/railway-record-framing, on origin), answering child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after codex's ratified changes-requested. Replaces artifact 4ac1bc77 at head 5f3bc441.\n\nThe blocking finding was that I had rebuilt something the package already had, and built it worse. internal/gitstore/audit.go carries AuditBatch.readObject: it validates the id, type, size, bounds and terminator, recomputes the object hash so the bytes are provably the object named, and poisons the reader when the stream desynchronises. My hand-rolled reader did none of the hash recomputation, so it accepted the id 1111... paired with the payload \"hello\", which actually hashes to 34f5fae8.\n\nThat means my previous claim was still false. Over three rounds I moved from \"did I see the bad byte\" to \"did I see a response for this hash\", and the question was always \"are these the bytes that hash to it\". Each revision was closer and none arrived, because I kept writing the check rather than looking for it.\n\nGraph now reads through AuditBatch and scans the returned exact payload for NUL. runExact and the hand-rolled framing parser are deleted.\n\nTwo things were tightened in the shared reader while reusing it, both from codex's list. Its header split used strings.Fields, which accepts a tab or a run of spaces wherever the cat-file protocol specifies exactly one; a reader more permissive than the protocol it validates cannot prove a stream is well formed. And the header check was buried in the read loop, so its refusals could only be reached by staging a damaged repository. parseCatFileHeader is now separable and eight exact-syntax cases test it directly: tab-separated, doubled spaces, trailing space, wrong id, wrong type, non-numeric size, no fields, and the exact protocol.\n\nMutations, each verified to compile before the count was believed: reverting to strings.Fields fails 4 tests, dropping the size bound fails 2, disabling the NUL scan fails 1, and removing object verification entirely fails 1. Two of those mutations did not compile on first attempt - \"bytes imported and not used\", \"content declared and not used\" - and I caught that rather than reading zero failures as coverage.\n\nThe general lesson, recorded because it is more useful than the fix. This morning I wrote down that before evaluating revived work you must ask whether main already does it. I applied that to a feature and not to infrastructure: I needed a framed-object reader and never grepped the package I was editing for one. Duplicating security-critical code is worse than duplicating ordinary code, because the copy inherits none of the original's checks and nobody reviewing the copy knows which are missing.\n\nOne correction on this head's own history. An intermediate amend left a commit message describing runExact after I had deleted it, because a scripted edit failed while the amend still ran. I rewrote the message rather than leave a commit asserting the presence of code that is not there.\n\nThe Git-versus-argv ref-name attribution is unchanged and still split by boundary.\n\nGates at this exact head: go test ./... 29 packages ok, go vet clean, make docs clean, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, branch pushed.\n\n5f3bc441 is not merged and artifact 4ac1bc77 is retired.","body":{"commit":"f61340c01c157bf82cbddf3792fa3075e924d362","path":"internal/gitstore"}}